Alteryx workflow walkthrough script
1. Manual entries
Show the yellow manual-entry container.
Say:
“The workflow begins with the manual-entry section. Here, we update the values required for the current campaign run, such as the report month, campaign start date, campaign end date, and user story number.
These values are used throughout the workflow and also help create the final output file names.”
Do not explain each tool. Just point to the input and select tools.
2. Initial member population
Show the initial population SQL and input section.
Say:
“Next, the workflow retrieves the initial member population from the database.
The SQL query applies the basic eligibility criteria and brings in the required member, provider, contract, market, and contact information.
The different source tables are joined together using identifiers such as GMPI ID, member ID, and provider ID.
After the population is retrieved, the workflow selects the required fields and removes duplicate records so that each member is processed correctly.”
You can briefly mention:
“This is our starting population before any suppressions are applied.”
3. Phone-number validation
Show the bad phone-number container.
Say:
“The next section validates the member phone number.
First, the workflow cleans the phone number by removing spaces, dashes, brackets, or other formatting characters.
It then checks whether the phone number is present, whether it contains ten digits, and whether it matches the expected phone-number format.
It also excludes obvious invalid values, such as repeated digits or restricted prefixes.
Members who fail these checks are categorized under ‘Bad Phone Numbers’ and are excluded from the campaign target population.”
4. Gryphon RND suppression
Show the pink Gryphon RND container.
Say:
“After the phone validation, the eligible population is compared with the Gryphon RND list.
RND refers to records that should not be contacted.
The workflow retrieves the RND records from the suppression table and joins them with the member population using GMPI ID.
Members who match the Gryphon RND list are removed from the campaign and added to the suppression summary.”
Avoid defining the acronym more specifically unless your team has confirmed its official meaning.
5. Gryphon Litigation suppression
Show the green Gryphon Litigation container.
Say:
“The next suppression check is the Gryphon Litigation list.
The workflow retrieves records where the litigation indicator is set to true and compares those members with the campaign population using GMPI ID.
Any matching members are excluded from SMS outreach and categorized as Gryphon Litigation suppressions.”
6. NANPA area-code suppression
Show the orange area-code container.
Say:
“Next, the workflow validates the member’s phone area code.
It extracts the first three digits of the phone number and creates a member area-code field.
That area code is compared with the NANPA area-code reference table.
Invalid, inactive, unsupported, or non-US area codes are suppressed so that the campaign includes only eligible phone numbers.”
You can keep NANPA simple:
“NANPA is the area-code reference used for North American phone numbers.”
7. SMS opt-out suppression
Show the blue opt-out container.
Say:
“The next check is the SMS opt-out suppression.
The workflow reads the opt-out records that were generated and loaded in the previous process.
Those records are matched with the current member population using GMPI ID.
If a member appears in the opt-out table, that member is excluded from the campaign.
This ensures that members who have unsubscribed do not receive any future SMS communications.”
This connects nicely to the slides you already prepared for SMS opt-out.
8. Claims and no-claims classification
Show the no-claims container.
Say:
“After the suppression checks, the workflow also identifies whether the member has a claim or does not have a claim.
A database query is used to retrieve the claim information for the selected reporting month.
The data is joined using member ID and GMPI ID.
Members are then assigned a status of either ‘Claim’ or ‘No Claim.’
These two streams are combined again so the final output includes the required member status.”
A small caution: explain this as classification unless the workflow truly suppresses one of the two groups.
9. Final campaign target file
Show the Campaign Target output container.
Say:
“Once all the suppression rules are complete, the remaining eligible population becomes the Campaign Target population.
The workflow adds standard campaign fields such as business unit, vendor, outreach mode, program information, campaign date, and tracking fields.
It then selects the required output columns and writes the Campaign Target file as a CSV.”
You can add:
“This is the file used for the actual SMS campaign execution.”
10. Business Review file
Show the Business Review output.
Say:
“The workflow also generates a Business Review file.
This file contains the detailed campaign population and supporting member information that can be reviewed before campaign execution.
The file name is created dynamically using the campaign date, and the output is written as a CSV.”
11. Suppression Summary
Show the formula descriptions, union, summarize, sort, and output tools.
Say:
“The last section creates the Suppression Summary.
Each branch of the workflow is given a description, such as Initial Query Population, Bad Phone Numbers, Gryphon RND, Gryphon Litigation, NANPA Area Codes, SMS Opt-Out Members, Members with Claims, and Members with No Claims.
All these streams are combined using the Union tool.
The workflow then groups the data by suppression description and counts the number of distinct members in each category.
The results are arranged in the required order and written to the Suppression Summary CSV.”
Closing statement
You can finish with:
“So, in summary, this workflow starts with the eligible member population, applies all required validation and suppression checks, classifies the members, and generates three final outputs: the Campaign Target file, the Business Review file, and the Suppression Summary.
These files are reviewed and then used in the next step to execute the SMS campaign.”
